<section xml:id="section-finite-fields">
  <title>Finite Fields</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-characteristic">
    <title>Characteristic of a Field</title>
    <p>
      I've left out one important definition for fields. I'll state it
      now, as it serves as good introduction to finite fields.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>K</m> be a field. Let <m>p</m> be the smallest positive
          integer such that <m>p1 = 0</m> in <m>K</m>, if it exists.
          (That is, 1 added to itself <m>p</m> times is 0). This number
          is called the <term>characteristic</term> of the field. If no
          such number exists, the characteristic of the field is
          infinity. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Characteristic is a very strange definition. It feels like a zero
      divisor: an equation written <m>p1 = 0</m>, which doesn't make
      much sense. Fields don't have zero divisors. But this isn't
      actually a zero divisor: in a field of characteristic p, there is
      no element <sq><m>q</m></sq>. This notation really does mean
      adding 1 to itself <m>p</m> times, not multiplying by some other
      element. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is best illustrated by the most important example of a field
      of characteristic <m>p</m>. Here is a new definition of a very
      familiar object. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p</m> be a prime number. The field <m>\FF_p</m> is
          the new notation for the familiar algebraic object
          <m>\ZZ/p\ZZ</m> when considered as a field. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      In <m>\FF_p</m>, it's explicitly true that <m> p = 0 \mod p</m>.
      Therefore, the equation <m>p1=0</m> make sense. This is a field of
      characteristic <m>p</m>. You can use this a more general sense
      of characterstic: a field of finite characteristic <m>p</m> has
      some version of arithmetic <sq>modulo <m>p</m></sq> in its
      strucutre. Hopefully this makes the notion of characteristic
      feel a bit less bizzare. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Fields of finite characteristic have some interesting and useful
      properties. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>K</m> be a field of characteristic <m>p</m>. Let
          <m>a, b, \K</m>. Then the following equation is true.
          <me>
            (a + b)^p = a^p + b^p 
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I can prove this with the binomial theorem. Let me expand
          the binomial on the left. 
          <me>
            (a + b)^p = a^p + pa^{p-1}b + {p\choose 2} a^{p-2}b^2 +
            \ldots + {p \choose p-2} a^2 b^{p-2} + p ab^{p-1} + b^p
          </me>
          Let me also remind you of the factorial description of the
          binomial coefficients.
          <me>
            {n \choose k} = \frac{n!}{k!(k-1)!}
          <me>
          If the <m>n = p</m> in this coefficient is prime and
          <m>k</m> is neither <m>n</m> or <m>0</m>, then <m>p</m>
          divides the numerator but not the denominator, since all the
          factors in the denominator are whole numbers strictly less
          than the prime. Therefore, in the expansion I wrote earlier,
          all of the middle binomial coefficients are multiples of
          <m>p</m>. But in a field of characteristic <m>p</m>, <m>p =
          0</m>. Therefore, all the middle binomial coefficients
          disappear. 
          <me>
            (a + b)^p = a^p + 0 + 0 + \ldots + 0 + 0 + b^p
          </me>
          This completes the proof. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      I can use the previous proposition to make the following
      definition.  
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>K</m> be a field of characteristic <m>p</m>. Then the
          <term>Forbenius endomorphism<term> is the homorphism
          <m>\phi: K \rightarrow K</m> defined as follows.
          <me>
            \phi(a) = a^p
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The Frobenius map is a field homomorphism because of the
      property <m>(a + b)^p = a^p + b^p</m>: this property shows that
      the function does, in fact, cooperate with addition. This is
      obviously not true unless the field has precisely the right
      characteristic. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-prime-subfields">
    <title>Prime Subfields</title>
    <p>
      Having defined characteristic and some of implications of finite
      characteristic, let me state and prove one of the basic truths
      of field theory. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>K</m> be field. Then there exists a unique minimal
          subfield of <m>K</m> called the <term>prime subfield</term>. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              
            </li>
            <li>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
</section>
